From 03a4a2b71b148a36b4428d84e380fcac2ee486aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 075/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: recover from firmware
 decode stalls in place

Some streams stop the HEVC firmware mid-slice: it never raises an
interrupt and never advances, so the session hangs until userspace
gives up.  Clients typically respond by flushing and restarting the
decoder, which historically drove the hardware through the very
transitions that could wedge the SoC.  The firmware's own timeout
status is not raised by the HEVC microcode, so the watchdog has to
live in the driver - as it does in the vendor driver.

Arm a watchdog whenever the driver hands work to the firmware and
disarm it when the interrupt arrives, so only firmware-owned time is
measured.  A stall is declared after 200 ms in any state where the
firmware owes the next action; while it is decoding slice data, an
additional grace period requires the LCU counter to stand still.  A
late interrupt racing a claimed stall is discarded.

Recovery halts the processors with full DMA and write-response drains,
preserves the stream position across the reset bracket, drops only the
poisoned in-flight frame (returning its buffer to the queue), re-runs
protocol and buffer-table initialisation without reallocating, and
sends the firmware back into NAL search.  It resumes at the next IRAP
with no session teardown - clients see a brief hiccup instead of a
dead decoder.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
